% primitives.elpi: what elpi-debug needs to know about the unification
% variables of a run, as a small interface of primitives that Elpi's core
% could offer (the wish list below, and ../ELPI-WISHLIST.md), written here
% in Elpi as STAND-INS until Elpi has them.  The other files of the
% interpreter go through this interface only: switching to the real
% builtins touches this file only.
%
% THE WISH LIST
%
% 1. [vars-of T Vs]: Vs are the unification variables occurring in T, each
%    once, in the order of their first occurrence (left to right, depth
%    first), under binders and inside applications of builtins.  A
%    variable applied to arguments (X a b) is given by its head X.  It
%    binds nothing.
%
% 2. [uuid X N]: N identifies the unification variable X for the whole
%    run: two variables have different numbers, and a variable keeps its
%    number at every event and across backtracking.  X must be a variable
%    (it fails otherwise); a variable bound to another one has the number
%    of that one (Elpi dereferences it).  elpi-debug names X "X<N>" in all
%    it shows ([uuid-name], [term-string]: the printer naming each
%    variable so).
%
% 3. The trail.  [trail-mark M]: M marks the current point of the trail.
%    [bindings-since M Bs]: Bs are the bindings made since M and still in
%    force, one [binding N V P] per variable that existed at M and is
%    bound now: N its uuid, V its value now, P the pruning Elpi made when
%    it bound it: [not-pruned]; [pruned-arguments U K N], it was replaced
%    by the variable of uuid U applied to K of its N arguments;
%    [pruned-scope Es], by a variable that may no longer depend on the
%    eigenvariables Es.  The variables created after M are not listed:
%    they are parts of the values.  (The order of Bs: the stand-in gives
%    the order of vars-of at the mark; the real builtin may give the order
%    of the trail.)  [debug-unif T1 T2 Bs] (mark, T1 = T2, bindings since
%    the mark) is not needed by elpi-debug: its head unification is split
%    between Elpi's own matching and the goals of compiled.elpi.
%
% THE STAND-INS (below), and their limits
%
% - vars-of walks the term ([shape-of], lprolog.elpi).  Elpi has no
%   builtin to take apart an application of a builtin (X is Y + 1 is one
%   node), so the walk does not see the variables there; nor those of a
%   conjunction of more than 16 goals.  Hence reflect's option -closed:
%   the debugger cannot abstract the variables of a rule itself, reflect
%   gives each rule closed (compiled.elpi).  The walk gets the head of an
%   applied variable with the builtin var; on a variable that Elpi keeps
%   in a compact form (G x, x the binder above, printed X0^1 by Elpi) that
%   head is a new variable, bound in place of the old one: from then on
%   the variable shows under a second number.
% - uuid reads the number Elpi's printer gives the variable.  The printer
%   keeps, for the whole run, a table from Elpi's internal identity of a
%   variable to a number given at its first print ("X12", "X12^1" for a
%   variable created under one binder): it is the one place where Elpi
%   lets a program see that identity.  A table kept by the debugger
%   cannot do it: a safe refuses a term with variables ("expected: closed
%   term"), local clauses do not outlive the goal that adds them, and
%   same_var cannot tell a variable from one bound to it (both dereference
%   to the same).  The stand-in is wrong inside a copy (std.findall copies
%   its goal and prints with a table of its own, from X0): the debugger
%   names no variable inside std.findall.
% - trail-mark cannot see the trail: it takes a snapshot of the variables
%   in reach, which it is told ([trail-mark Reach M], Reach a closure that
%   gives them; the real builtin needs no Reach and would not call it),
%   and bindings-since compares them with what they are now: O(variables
%   in reach) at the mark and again at bindings-since.  What is in reach
%   of a goal: the variables of the goal, and those of the hypothetical
%   clauses in scope (a clause added by => shares its variables with the
%   goal that added it; the frames above give no other access).  Missed:
%   a variable reached only through a suspended goal of the constraint
%   store (CHR), or inside an application of a builtin (vars-of above).
%   The eigenvariables a variable may depend on are found by trying to
%   bind it to each one (can-depend, lprolog.elpi).
%
% Nothing here runs on the fast path (no event shown or recorded).


% ===== 1. The variables of a term ==============================================

% [vars-of T Vs]: the unification variables of T (stand-in: the walk of
% [shape-of], which does not look into applications of builtins).
pred vars-of i:any, o:list any.
vars-of T Vs :- vars-acc T [] Rev, std.rev Rev Vs.

% [vars-acc T Acc Acc1]: Acc1 is Acc and, most recent first, the variables
% of T not in Acc.
pred vars-acc i:any, i:list any, o:list any.
vars-acc T Acc Acc1 :- shape-of T S, shape-vars S Acc Acc1.

pred shape-vars i:shape, i:list any, o:list any.
shape-vars (flexible H) Acc Acc :- std.exists Acc (same_var H), !.
shape-vars (flexible H) Acc [H|Acc].
shape-vars leaf Acc Acc.
shape-vars (node _ Ts) Acc Acc1 :- vars-acc-all Ts Acc Acc1.
shape-vars (binder F) Acc Acc1 :- pi x\ sigma B\ binder-body F x B, vars-acc B Acc Acc1.

pred vars-acc-all i:list any, i:list any, o:list any.
vars-acc-all [] Acc Acc.
vars-acc-all [T|Ts] Acc Acc2 :- vars-acc T Acc Acc1, vars-acc-all Ts Acc1 Acc2.

% ===== 2. The identity of a variable ================================================

% [uuid X N]: the number of the variable X for the whole run (stand-in:
% the number of Elpi's printer, "X12^1" for 12).
pred uuid i:any, o:int.
uuid X N :- var X H _, term_to_string H S, printer-number S N.

% [printer-number S N]: S is the printer's name of a variable, "X<N>"
% followed by its depth ("^1") if any.
pred printer-number i:string, o:int.
printer-number S N :- rex.match "^X[0-9]+" S, rex.replace "^X\\([0-9]+\\).*$" "\\1" S Digits, N is string_to_int Digits.

% [uuid-name X S]: "X<uuid>", the name elpi-debug shows for the variable X.
func uuid-name any -> string.
uuid-name X S :- uuid X N, !, uuid-label N S.
uuid-name X S :- term-string X S.       % (not a variable: printed)

% [uuid-label N S]: "X<N>", the name of the variable of uuid N.
func uuid-label int -> string.
uuid-label N S :- S is "X" ^ {std.any->string N}.

% [term-string T S]: T printed, each of its variables named X<uuid>
% (stand-in: the printer's text, whose numbers are the uuids, without the
% depth of each variable, "X12^1" printed X12).  The real builtin would be
% the printer naming each variable by its identity (Elpi's printer has
% the line, commented out: "X" ^ string_of_int (uvar_id r)).
func term-string any -> string.
term-string T S :- term_to_string T S0, rex.replace "\\bX\\([0-9]+\\)\\^[0-9]+" "X\\1" S0 S.

% ===== 3. The trail ========================================================================

% A mark of the trail (stand-in: a snapshot of the variables in reach).
data trail-mark.
symb snapshot list var-snapshot -> trail-mark.

% A variable at the mark: itself, its uuid, and the eigenvariables it may
% depend on (to tell a pruning of its scope).
data var-snapshot.
symb var-snapshot any -> int -> list any -> var-snapshot.

% A binding made since a mark: the uuid of the variable, its value now,
% and the pruning Elpi made when it bound it.
data binding.
symb binding int -> any -> pruning -> binding.

data pruning.
symb not-pruned pruning.
symb pruned-arguments int -> int -> int -> pruning.  % the variable of uuid U gets K of the N arguments
symb pruned-scope list any -> pruning.               % may no longer depend on these eigenvariables

% [trail-mark Reach M]: a mark of the trail now.  Reach R gives a term R
% holding the variables that the goals run until bindings-since may bind:
% the stand-in needs it, the real builtin would not call it.
pred trail-mark i:(pred o:any), o:trail-mark.
trail-mark Reach (snapshot Ss) :- Reach R, vars-of R Vs, std.map Vs snapshot-of Ss.

pred snapshot-of i:any, o:var-snapshot.
snapshot-of V (var-snapshot V N Vis) :- uuid V N, visible-eigens V Vis.

% [bindings-since M Bs]: the bindings made since the mark M, still in
% force (stand-in: each variable of the snapshot that is no longer
% itself).
pred bindings-since i:trail-mark, o:list binding.
bindings-since (snapshot Ss) Bs :- bindings-of-snapshots Ss Bs.

pred bindings-of-snapshots i:list var-snapshot, o:list binding.
bindings-of-snapshots [] [].
bindings-of-snapshots [var-snapshot V N Vis | Ss] [binding N V P | Bs] :- bound-since V N, !,
  pruning-of V Vis P,
  bindings-of-snapshots Ss Bs.
bindings-of-snapshots [_ | Ss] Bs :- bindings-of-snapshots Ss Bs.

% [bound-since V N]: the variable of uuid N, V, is bound: it is now a
% term, or (headed by) another variable.
pred bound-since i:any, i:int.
bound-since V N :- not (uuid V N).

% [pruning-of V Vis P]: what Elpi pruned when it bound V: V is now
% x1..xn\ H a1..ak with k < n (H does not get all the arguments), or V
% is now a variable (applied or not) that may depend on fewer
% eigenvariables than Vis, those V might depend on at the mark.
pred pruning-of i:any, i:list any, o:pruning.
pruning-of V _ (pruned-arguments U K N) :- lambda-prefix V 0 N K U, N > 0, K < N, !.
pruning-of V Vis (pruned-scope Lost) :- var V, visible-eigens V Now, lost-eigens Vis Now Lost, not (Lost = []), !.
pruning-of _ _ not-pruned.

% [lambda-prefix T N0 N K U]: T is N-N0 binders around H a1..ak, H the
% variable of uuid U, k = K; the test is made under the binders, where
% the body lives.  (The builtin var is asked for the arguments only: asked
% for the head and the arguments together, it binds the variable to a new
% one, which would then show under another number.)
pred lambda-prefix i:any, i:int, o:int, o:int, o:int.
lambda-prefix T N0 N K U :- shape-of T S, S = binder F, !, N1 is N0 + 1,
  pi x\ sigma B\ binder-body F x B, lambda-prefix B N1 N K U.
lambda-prefix T N N K U :- var T _ Args, std.length Args K, uuid T U.

% [lost-eigens Before Now Lost]: the eigenvariables of Before not in Now.
pred lost-eigens i:list any, i:list any, o:list any.
lost-eigens [] _ [].
lost-eigens [E|Es] Now Lost :- std.exists Now (same_term E), !, lost-eigens Es Now Lost.
lost-eigens [E|Es] Now [E|Lost] :- lost-eigens Es Now Lost.
